  "response": "```python\nimport heapq\n\ndef shortest_path(edges, start, end):\n    # If start and end are the same node\n    if start == end:\n        return (0, [start])\n    \n    # Build adjacency list graph\n    graph = {}\n    for from_node, to_node, weight in edges:\n        if from_node not in graph:\n            graph[from_node] = {}\n        graph[from_node][to_node] = weight\n    \n    # Check for negative weights\n    for from_node, to_node, weight in edges:\n        if weight < 0:\n            raise ValueError(\"Negative edge weight detected\")\n    \n    # Dijkstra's algorithm\n    distances = {node: float('inf') for node in graph}\n    distances[start] = 0\n    previous = {node: None for node in graph}\n    previous[end] = None\n    pq = [(0, start)]\n    visited = set()\n    \n    while pq:\n        current_dist, current_node = heapq.heappop(pq)\n        \n        # Skip if already processed with shorter distance\n        if current_node in visited or current_dist > distances.get(current_node, float('inf')):\n            continue\n        visited.add(current_node)\n        \n        # Early exit if we reached the destination\n        if current_node == end:\n            break\n        \n        # Explore neighbors\n        if current_node in graph:\n            for neighbor, weight in graph[current_node].items():\n                new_dist = current_dist + weight\n                \n                # Update if shorter path found\n                if new_dist < distances.get(neighbor, float('inf')):\n                    distances[neighbor] = new_dist\n                    previous[neighbor] = current_node\n                    heapq.heappush(pq, (new_dist, neighbor))\n    \n    # If no path exists\n    if distances.get(end) == float('inf') or end not in graph:\n        return (None, None)\n    \n    # Reconstruct path\n    path = []\n    node = end\n    while node is not None:\n        path.append(node)\n        node = previous[node]\n    \n    path.reverse()\n    \n    return (distances[end], path)\n```\n\n### Example Usage\n```python\nedges = [(\"A\",\"B\",1), (\"B\",\"C\",2), (\"A\",\"C\",5)]\nstart, end = \"A\", \"C\"\n\nprint(shortest_path(edges, start, end))  # Output: (3, ['A', 'B', 'C'])\n```\n\n### Notes\n- This implementation uses Dijkstra’s algorithm with a priority queue to efficiently find the shortest path in graphs with non-negative edge weights.\n- It reconstructs the path by tracking previous nodes during traversal.\n- Negative edge weights trigger an error, as they're not supported by Dijkstra's algorithm.\n- For disconnected graphs or unreachable destinations, it returns `(None, None)`.",
